        "language": "javascript",
        "explanation": "Hashed Sharding involves creating a Hashed Index on a field (usually `_id`) and using that as the shard key. MongoDB calculates the MD5 hash of the field's value and uses that hash to determine the chunk and shard.",
        "example": "Distributing writes.",
        "code": "// User 1 (_id: 1) -> Hash: 0x9a3... -> Goes to Shard A\n// User 2 (_id: 2) -> Hash: 0x1b4... -> Goes to Shard C\n// User 3 (_id: 3) -> Hash: 0x8f2... -> Goes to Shard B\n\n// Writes are perfectly distributed across the cluster!",
        "tip": "Hashed sharding guarantees even data distribution, which is usually the primary goal of sharding in the first place.",
        "mistake": "Trying to do a range query (`$gt`, `$lt`) on a hashed shard key. The router will have to perform a Scatter-Gather query to all shards because the data is randomized.",
